1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,273
The brush uncovers a temple that is below the ground around it.

2
00:00:04,273 --> 00:00:06,680
Builders start at the bottom and go up.

3
00:00:10,767 --> 00:00:14,496
Whoever made this started at the top of a hillside and cut downward,

4
00:00:14,496 --> 00:00:19,900
removing the rock around the temple until the building was left standing in a courtyard of its own.

5
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:20,927
Nothing was added.

6
00:00:20,927 --> 00:00:22,687
Everything was taken away.

7
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:33,193
Inscriptions, which for this period are the backbone — copper plates and temple inscriptions from all three powers.

8
00:00:33,193 --> 00:00:38,369
Travellers' accounts, including Arab merchants and geographers writing about western India.

9
00:00:38,369 --> 00:00:43,676
And texts, including a Sanskrit poem that is one of our better sources for the politics.

10
00:00:46,367 --> 00:00:48,198
Three powers, and one city.

11
00:00:48,198 --> 00:00:51,075
The Palas in the east, in Bengal and Bihar.

12
00:00:51,075 --> 00:00:55,783
The Gurjara-Pratiharas in the west, in Rajasthan and the Gangetic west.

13
00:00:55,783 --> 00:00:58,176
The Rashtrakutas in the Deccan.

14
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:07,735
For roughly two centuries each of them tried to take Kannauj — Harsha's old capital, now the symbol of paramountcy over northern India.

15
00:01:07,735 --> 00:01:10,615
Hold the city and you were the pre-eminent power.

16
00:01:10,615 --> 00:01:12,816
That is the tripartite struggle.

17
00:01:15,333 --> 00:01:18,102
Each is worth knowing for more than the fighting.

18
00:01:18,102 --> 00:01:25,025
The Palas, founded by Gopala and strongest under Dharmapala and Devapala, were Buddhism's last great royal patrons in India:

19
00:01:25,025 --> 00:01:30,974
they endowed Nalanda and founded Vikramashila, and Buddhist scholars went from their courts to Tibet.

20
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:42,522
The Pratiharas held the west for a long time and are credited with resisting Arab expansion beyond Sindh.

21
00:01:42,522 --> 00:01:47,351
The Rashtrakutas, under rulers including Dhruva, Govinda the Third and Amoghavarsha,

22
00:01:47,351 --> 00:01:53,161
were the most frequently successful of the three in the north, and the most interesting at home.

23
00:01:54,867 --> 00:01:59,863
And the Rashtrakutas built Kailasa at Ellora, which is the monument of this episode.

24
00:01:59,863 --> 00:02:04,566
Cut downward out of the hillside in the eighth century under Krishna the First,

25
00:02:04,566 --> 00:02:10,450
it is a complete temple — tower, hall, gateway, courtyard — excavated rather than constructed.

26
00:02:12,533 --> 00:02:17,677
Ellora also holds Buddhist, Jain and Hindu caves side by side on the same hillside,

27
00:02:17,677 --> 00:02:22,018
which is worth noticing: three traditions patronised at one site.

28
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:24,924
So how does it end?

29
00:02:24,924 --> 00:02:25,741
Nobody wins.

30
00:02:25,741 --> 00:02:30,895
Kannauj changes hands repeatedly; the Rashtrakutas raid north and withdraw south;

31
00:02:30,895 --> 00:02:37,355
the Pratiharas hold it longest and then fragment; the Palas decline and revive and decline again.

32
00:02:42,367 --> 00:02:49,745
By the tenth century all three are weakening and their own feudatories are becoming independent kingdoms — the Rajput houses in the west,

33
00:02:49,745 --> 00:02:53,702
the Chandelas, the Paramaras, and in the Deccan the Chalukyas of Kalyani.

34
00:02:53,702 --> 00:02:57,959
The struggle's real outcome is not a winner but a map of regional states.

35
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:03,135
A caution about how this is often told.

36
00:03:03,135 --> 00:03:10,064
The common account says the three wore each other out, left the north divided, and so prepared the way for later invasions.

37
00:03:10,064 --> 00:03:11,817
There is something in it.

38
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:27,777
But the more careful reading points out that each power declined for reasons of its own — succession disputes, pressure from feudatories, regional revolts — that the period produced major temples,

39
00:03:27,777 --> 00:03:30,108
literature and learning rather than decay,

40
00:03:30,108 --> 00:03:36,287
and that treating two centuries of history as a prelude to someone else's arrival is a poor way to understand it.

41
00:03:36,287 --> 00:03:39,351
Use the narrative; do not let it do your thinking.

42
00:03:41,233 --> 00:03:44,245
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

43
00:03:44,245 --> 00:03:49,688
Pala east, Pratihara west, Rashtrakuta south — all three for Kannauj.

44
00:03:53,633 --> 00:03:54,427
Dig Quiz.

45
00:03:54,427 --> 00:03:55,936
Pause and name it.

46
00:03:55,936 --> 00:04:00,065
First: the city all three powers fought to control.

47
00:04:00,065 --> 00:04:01,080
Kannauj.

48
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:09,203
Second: the monastery founded by the Palas, alongside their patronage of Nalanda.

49
00:04:09,203 --> 00:04:10,092
Vikramashila.

50
00:04:10,092 --> 00:04:15,785
Third: the temple at Ellora cut downward out of the hillside under the Rashtrakutas.

51
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:18,868
Kailasa.

52
00:04:18,868 --> 00:04:24,596
In Mains, a standard question asks about the consequences of the tripartite struggle.

53
00:04:26,033 --> 00:04:30,361
Give the conventional answer, then complicate it: regional states formed,

54
00:04:30,361 --> 00:04:34,404
and the period was culturally productive, not simply a decline.

55
00:04:36,467 --> 00:04:42,738
Kailasa takes its place in the gallery, and the sixth room is full: a temple made by removing a hillside.

56
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:51,929
Next gallery: those regional states in their own right — not as the end of something, but as what came next.

57
00:04:51,929 --> 00:04:55,800
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

